          explain: 'Titan absolutely contains water ice (A is incorrect): Titan\'s surface is largely made of water ice bedrock, exposed in crater rims, mountain ranges, and as the rounded "pebbles" photographed by Huygens. The pebbles Huygens imaged are fragments of water ice smoothed by methane fluvial transport. Titan accreted significant water from the outer solar system during formation. Saturn\'s rings consist of water ice, not condensed atmospheric water from its moons (C contains errors); volatile species at Saturn\'s distance include not just hydrocarbons but also water ice, ammonia ice, and CO₂ ice. Tidal heating from Saturn does contribute to Titan\'s interior energy budget (sufficient to maintain a subsurface liquid water ocean), but it does not "evaporate water" at the surface (D); the surface temperature of 93.7 K is controlled primarily by solar irradiance and the greenhouse effect of the N₂/CH₄ atmosphere. The correct answer is B: the key is understanding phase diagrams. The methane cycle on Titan exists because methane\'s phase transition temperatures fall within Titan\'s environmental conditions. At Titan\'s surface pressure (1.45 bar), methane\'s boiling point is approximately 112 K (−161°C at 1 bar, shifting to ~115 K at 1.45 bar) and its freezing point is ~90.7 K. Titan\'s surface temperature of 93.7 K places methane just above its freezing point and at a temperature where its vapour pressure allows liquid-vapour exchange — methane can condense from clouds, form surface lakes, and evaporate. Water, at the same surface conditions, has a boiling point of ~395 K at 1.45 bar and a melting point of 273 K — both impossibly high for Titan\'s 93.7 K surface. Water ice at Titan\'s temperature is as rigid as granite (a material comparison valid because at −179°C, water ice has a Young\'s modulus comparable to cold rock). Liquid water on Titan\'s surface would require heating the surface by nearly 180°C above its current value — only achievable transiently in impact craters or cryovolcanic events.',
